Here is a complete, working example of defining routes in **AngularJS (v1.x)** using the `ngRoute` module.

In AngularJS, routing is handled via the `$routeProvider` service. You define your routes in the module configuration and specify which template and controller should load for each URL.

### Prerequisites

To use routing, you must include the `angular-route.js` library in your HTML.

```html
<script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/angularjs/1.8.2/angular.min.js"></script>
<script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/angularjs/1.8.2/angular-route.min.js"></script>
```

### Complete Example

Create a single file (e.g., `index.html`) or split the logic into separate files. Below is a self-contained example demonstrating a Home page, an About page, and a dynamic User page.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html ng-app="myApp">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>AngularJS Routing Example</title>
    <!-- Include AngularJS and the ngRoute module -->
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/angularjs/1.8.2/angular.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/angularjs/1.8.2/angular-route.min.js"></script>

    <style>
        nav { margin-bottom: 20px; }
        nav a { margin-right: 15px; text-decoration: none; color: #333; font-weight: bold; }
        nav a.active { color: blue; text-decoration: underline; }
        .container { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 20px; border-radius: 5px; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <!-- Navigation Bar -->
    <nav>
        <a href="#/">Home</a>
        <a href="#/about">About</a>
        <a href="#/user/123">User 123</a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Main Content Area -->
    <!-- The ng-view directive is where the templates will be injected -->
    <div class="container">
        <h2>Current Route: {{currentRoute}}</h2>
        <div ng-view></div>
    </div>

    <script>
        // 1. Define the App Module and inject 'ngRoute'
        var app = angular.module('myApp', ['ngRoute']);

        // 2. Configure the Routes
        app.config(function($routeProvider) {
            $routeProvider
                // Define a route for the root '/'
                .when('/', {
                    templateUrl: 'home.html', // Can also be a string: '<h1>Welcome Home</h1>'
                    controller: 'HomeController'
                })

                // Define a route for '/about'
                .when('/about', {
                    templateUrl: 'about.html',
                    controller: 'AboutController'
                })

                // Define a dynamic route with a parameter (e.g., /user/123)
                // ':id' captures the value after /user/
                .when('/user/:id', {
                    templateUrl: 'user.html',
                    controller: 'UserController',
                    resolve: {
                        // Optional: Run code before the route is fully loaded
                        userId: function($route) {
                            return $route.current.params.id;
                        }
                    }
                })

                // Optional: Define a default route (404)
                .otherwise({
                    redirectTo: '/'
                });
        });

        // 3. Define Controllers
        // Home Controller
        app.controller('HomeController', function($scope) {
            $scope.message = "Welcome to the Home Page!";
        });

        // About Controller
        app.controller('AboutController', function($scope) {
            $scope.message = "This is the About Page.";
        });

        // User Controller (uses dynamic parameter)
        app.controller('UserController', function($scope, $routeParams) {
            // $routeParams contains the parameters defined in the route (e.g., :id)
            $scope.userId = $routeParams.id;
            $scope.message = "You are viewing user with ID: " + $scope.userId;
        });

        // Optional: Track active route for navigation styling
        app.run(function($rootScope) {
            $rootScope.$on('$routeChangeSuccess', function(event, current) {
                $rootScope.currentRoute = current.$$route.originalPath;
            });
        });
    </script>

    <!-- 
      HTML Templates 
      In a real app, these would be external files (home.html, etc.).
      We are using <script type="text/ng-template"> to keep this example single-file.
    -->

    <script type="text/ng-template" id="home.html">
        <h1>Home Page</h1>
        <p ng-bind="message"></p>
    </script>

    <script type="text/ng-template" id="about.html">
        <h1>About Page</h1>
        <p ng-bind="message"></p>
    </script>

    <script type="text/ng-template" id="user.html">
        <h1>User Details</h1>
        <p>User ID: <strong>{{userId}}</strong></p>
        <p ng-bind="message"></p>
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Key Concepts Explained

1.  **Module Dependency**: When creating your app module (`angular.module('myApp', ...)`), you must add `'ngRoute'` to the dependency array. Without this, `$routeProvider` will not be available.
2.  **`$routeProvider.when()`**: This method defines a specific URL path and maps it to a `templateUrl` (HTML file or string) and a `controller`.
3.  **`ng-view`**: This is a directive placed in your main HTML layout. AngularJS injects the template associated with the current URL into this specific div.
4.  **Dynamic Parameters**: In `.when('/user/:id', ...)` the colon (`:`) indicates a parameter. Inside the controller, you access this value via the `$routeParams` service (e.g., `$routeParams.id`).
5.  **`ng-template`**: For this example, I used `<script type="text/ng-template" id="...">` to embed the HTML directly in the file. In a production environment, you would typically move these to separate files like `views/home.html` and just reference the filename in `templateUrl`.

### How to Run
1.  Save the code above as an `.html` file (e.g., `routing.html`).
2.  Open it in any web browser.
3.  Click the navigation links to see the content change without reloading the page.

Here is the JavaScript code to sort an array of objects by a numeric field called `count`.

The `Array.prototype.sort()` method accepts a comparison function. For numeric sorting, you should return the difference between the two values (`a.count - b.count` for ascending order).

### JavaScript Example

```javascript
// 1. Define the array of objects with the specific counts
const items = [
  { id: 1, name: "First", count: 48 },
  { id: 2, name: "Second", count: 55 },
  { id: 3, name: "Third", count: 61 }
];

// 2. Sort the array in Ascending order
// Formula: (a.count - b.count)
items.sort((a, b) => a.count - b.count);

console.log("Sorted (Ascending):", items);

// --- Optional: Sort in Descending Order ---
items.sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count);

console.log("Sorted (Descending):", items);
```

### Output Explanation

1.  **Ascending Order** (Default example above):
    The array will be ordered from smallest `count` to largest:
    ```javascript
    [
      { id: 1, name: "First", count: 48 },
      { id: 2, name: "Second", count: 55 },
      { id: 3, name: "Third", count: 61 }
    ]
    ```

2.  **Descending Order** (Optional example):
    If you reverse the subtraction (`b - a`), the order becomes:
    ```javascript
    [
      { id: 3, name: "Third", count: 61 },
      { id: 2, name: "Second", count: 55 },
      { id: 1, name: "First", count: 48 }
    ]
    ```

### Key Note
When sorting numbers in JavaScript, it is crucial to subtract the values (`a - b`) rather than comparing them directly (`a < b`). If you simply use a boolean comparison, JavaScript converts the result to a string for sorting, which can lead to incorrect results (e.g., treating numbers as text). Using the subtraction method ensures a proper numeric sort.
